# Refactor Model Switching Workflow

Before starting any refactor slice or sub-slice, the agent must first research the target code, then optimize for the cheapest capable model and ask for approval before coding.

## Required Sequence

1. Inspect the roadmap/codebase first.
2. Identify the exact slice and sub-slices.
3. Conduct read-only research of the target slice/sub-slice code before choosing a model.
4. Report the evidence: functions, line ranges, responsibilities, CC/CodeScene risk if available, and dependencies.
5. Present possible AI models for each sub-slice.
6. Recommend the cheapest capable model and explain why in 1 short sentence.
7. Ask Ezequiel before switching models or launching coding work.
8. Complete only the approved sub-slice.
9. Ask before moving to the next sub-slice.

## Required Research First

Do not pick a model from the roadmap alone. First inspect the actual code that will be edited.

The research report must include:

- target files and functions
- approximate line ranges
- what each function is responsible for
- complexity/risk evidence from CodeScene if available
- dependencies touched (`chrome.storage`, Supabase, DOM, shared feature state)
- whether the work is safe to parallelize or should be sequential

Only after this research may the agent propose models.

## Required Model Plan Format

After research and before coding, show a compact table:

| Sub-slice | Possible models | Recommended | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Example scaffolding | Sonnet 4.6, GPT 5.5 | Sonnet 4.6 | Cheapest capable for low-risk extraction |

The recommendation must favor the cheapest model that can safely do the work. Expensive models need a clear risk reason.

## Approval Prompts

Use direct checkpoint questions like:

- `Ezequiel, should I start this sub-slice with Sonnet 4.6?`
- `Ezequiel, should I switch this sub-slice to Opus 4.5?`
- `Ezequiel, should I switch this specific hard method to Opus 4.7?`
- `Ezequiel, should GPT 5.5 review this slice before commit?`
- `Ezequiel, should we move to the next sub-slice?`

Do not assume approval from prior context. Ask at each model boundary and each sub-slice boundary.

## Cost-Aware Model Ladder

- DeepSeek v4-Flash: cheapest for summaries, drafting, and high-volume simple tasks.
- Sonnet 4.6: default for scaffolding, selectors, constants, simple storage, simple UI extraction.
- GPT 5.5: planning, architecture review, final diff review, test/checklist generation.
- Opus 4.5: medium-complex methods, event-heavy decompositions, CC 40-70.
- DeepSeek v4-Pro: backend/Supabase sync modules and multi-table merge logic.
- Opus 4.7: most expensive; only for hardest methods, CC > 70, fragile AI/avatar/auth/sync logic.

## Model Choice Rules

- If Sonnet 4.6 can safely do it, recommend Sonnet 4.6.
- If the task is planning/review only, recommend GPT 5.5 unless a cheaper summary is enough.
- If the task is backend Supabase sync or multi-table merge logic, recommend DeepSeek v4-Pro.
- If the task is CC 40-70 or event-heavy decomposition, recommend Opus 4.5.
- If the task is CC > 70 or fragile AI/auth/avatar/sync logic, recommend Opus 4.7.
- Never choose Opus 4.7 just because it is strongest. Use it only when risk justifies the credit cost.

## Hard Rule

Do not start implementation just because the next slice is known. First research the real code, then present the model plan, then wait for Ezequiel to approve the first model/sub-slice.
